      "residency": "100% GPU",
      "cold_start": null,
      "runs": [
        {
          "model": "gemma4:26b",
          "ok": true,
          "error": null,
          "ttft_ms": 401.9989580000001,
          "ttfv_ms": 7827.945041,
          "wall_total_ms": 10939.019333000004,
          "load_ms": 304.107458,
          "total_ms": 10935.253416,
          "prompt_eval_ms": 43.42,
          "eval_ms": 10572.982,
          "prompt_tokens": 66,
          "output_tokens": 847,
          "gen_tps": 80.10984980396259,
          "prompt_tps": 1520.0368493781668,
          "response_text": "Quantizing an LLM from FP16 to INT4 impacts the model as follows:\n\n1.  **Memory Footprint: Significant Reduction**\n    FP16 uses 16 bits (2 bytes) per parameter, while INT4 uses only 4 bits (0.5 bytes). This reduces the VRAM required to store the model weights by approximately **4x**, allowing much larger models to fit on consumer-grade GPUs.\n\n2.  **Inference Throughput: Significant Increase**\n    LLM inference is typically \"memory-bandwidth bound,\" meaning the bottleneck is how fast data moves from VRAM to the processor. Because INT4 requires transferring 75% less data per parameter, the speed of token generation (tokens per second) increases substantially.\n\n3.  **Output Quality: Slight Degradation**\n    Quantization introduces \"quantization error\" because continuous values are rounded into discrete 4-bit bins. This loss of precision can increase perplexity and lead to a measurable drop in reasoning capabilities or factual accuracy. However, modern techniques like **GPTQ** or **AWQ** use calibration to minimize this loss, making the quality degradation often negligible for many use cases.",
          "thinking_chars": 2409,
          "approximate_tokens": false,
          "stream_chunks": null,
